# Efficient Collision Detection

## Context
- When implementing physics and collision in games with many entities
- When experiencing performance drops during collision-heavy scenarios
- When using custom collision shapes beyond Phaser's built-in circles and rectangles
- When implementing line-of-sight or detection mechanics

## Requirements
- Use Phaser's built-in collision groups to batch similar physics objects
- Implement spatial partitioning via Phaser's internal tree for custom collision systems
- Use a broad-phase (distance check) before detailed collision calculations
- Create collision layers and categories to filter unnecessary collision checks
- Disable physics for off-screen objects or those far from interaction
- Use appropriate physics body types (static, dynamic) based on object behavior
- Profile collision hotspots and optimize the most frequently called routines

## Examples

<example>
```typescript
// Using Phaser's optimized collision detection with groups and layers
export class OptimizedCollisionManager {
  private scene: Phaser.Scene;
  private player: Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Sprite;
  private enemies: Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Group;
  private projectiles: Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Group;
  private obstacles: Phaser.Physics.Arcade.StaticGroup;
  
  constructor(scene: Phaser.Scene, player: Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Sprite) {
    this.scene = scene;
    this.player = player;
    
    // Create physics groups with appropriate configs
    this.enemies = scene.physics.add.group({
      collideWorldBounds: true,
      bounceX: 0.5,
      bounceY: 0.5
    });
    
    this.projectiles = scene.physics.add.group({
      allowGravity: false
    });
    
    // Static bodies for immovable objects (better performance)
    this.obstacles = scene.physics.add.staticGroup();
    
    // Set up collision handlers with specific callbacks
    this.setupCollisions();
  }
  
  setupCollisions(): void {
    // Player collides with obstacles - no callback needed
    this.scene.physics.add.collider(this.player, this.obstacles);
    
    // Enemies collide with obstacles and each other
    this.scene.physics.add.collider(this.enemies, this.obstacles);
    this.scene.physics.add.collider(this.enemies, this.enemies);
    
    // Projectiles only overlap with enemies (one-way collision)
    this.scene.physics.add.overlap(
      this.projectiles, 
      this.enemies, 
      this.handleProjectileEnemyCollision,
      this.filterActiveObjects, // Process function for early filtering
      this
    );
    
    // Optimize player-enemy collision with custom processing
    this.scene.physics.add.overlap(
      this.enemies,
      this.player,
      this.handleEnemyPlayerCollision,
      this.filterEnemyPlayerCollision, // Only check active attacking enemies
      this
    );
  }
  
  // Process function to filter inactive objects before expensive collision checks
  filterActiveObjects(obj1: any, obj2: any): boolean {
    return obj1.active && obj2.active;
  }
  
  // More specific filtering based on game state and distance
  filterEnemyPlayerCollision(enemy: any, player: any): boolean {
    // First check if both are active
    if (!enemy.active || !player.active) return false;
    
    // Then do a rough distance check before detailed collision
    const dx = enemy.x - player.x;
    const dy = enemy.y - player.y;
    const distSquared = dx * dx + dy * dy;
    
    // Only check collision if enemy is close enough (avoid sqrt)
    return distSquared < 10000; // 100 pixel radius
  }
  
  update(time: number, delta: number): void {
    // Disable physics for enemies far from player
    this.enemies.getChildren().forEach((enemy: any) => {
      const dx = enemy.x - this.player.x;
      const dy = enemy.y - this.player.y;
      const distSquared = dx * dx + dy * dy;
      
      // Enable/disable physics based on distance
      if (distSquared > 250000) { // 500 pixel radius
        enemy.body.enable = false;
      } else if (!enemy.body.enable) {
        enemy.body.enable = true;
      }
    });
  }
  
  createComplexCollider(): void {
    // For irregular shapes, use compound bodies
    const enemy = this.enemies.create(400, 300, 'enemy');
    
    // Create a compound body from multiple shapes
    const body = enemy.body as Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Body;
    body.setSize(30, 50); // Core body
    body.setOffset(10, 15); // Alignment with sprite
  }
}
```
This example demonstrates efficient Phaser collision handling using physics groups, optimized filtering with process callbacks, distance-based optimization, and proper static vs. dynamic body usage.
</example>

<example type="invalid">
```typescript
// Inefficient collision detection
export class UnoptimizedCollisionManager {
  private scene: Phaser.Scene;
  private player: Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Sprite;
  private enemies: Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Sprite[] = [];
  private projectiles: Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Sprite[] = [];
  
  constructor(scene: Phaser.Scene, player: Phaser.Physics.Arcade.Sprite) {
    this.scene = scene;
    this.player = player;
  }
  
  update(): void {
    // Check ALL projectiles against ALL enemies every frame (O(n²) complexity)
    for (const projectile of this.projectiles) {
      if (!projectile.active) continue;
      
      for (const enemy of this.enemies) {
        if (!enemy.active) continue;
        
        // Expensive rectangle collision check every frame
        if (Phaser.Geom.Intersects.RectangleToRectangle(
          projectile.getBounds(),
          enemy.getBounds()
        )) {
          this.handleProjectileEnemyCollision(projectile, enemy);
        }
      }
    }
    
    // No spatial partitioning or distance-based filtering
    // No use of Phaser's optimized collision system
    // No grouping of similar objects for batch processing
  }
}
```
This approach manually checks all objects against each other, resulting in O(n²) complexity and poor performance as object count increases. It doesn't use Phaser's built-in collision system or any spatial partitioning.
</example>

## Phaser-Specific Notes

1. Use Phaser's collision categories for complex filtering:
   ```typescript
   // Setup physics categories
   const CATEGORY_PLAYER = 0x0001;
   const CATEGORY_ENEMY = 0x0002;
   const CATEGORY_BULLET = 0x0004;
   const CATEGORY_WALL = 0x0008;
   
   // Set player to collide only with walls and enemies
   player.body.setCollidesWith([CATEGORY_WALL, CATEGORY_ENEMY]);
   
   // Set bullets to collide only with enemies
   bullet.body.setCollidesWith([CATEGORY_ENEMY]);
   ```

2. Leverage Phaser's built-in spatial hash:
   ```typescript
   // Get all bodies in a specific region (much faster than checking all)
   const bodiesInArea = this.scene.physics.world.query.rectangle(x, y, width, height);
   
   // Process only nearby bodies
   bodiesInArea.forEach(body => {
     // Handle collisions with bodies in this region
   });
   ```

3. Use Phaser's collision groups for multi-object collision callbacks:
   ```typescript
   // Create collision group
   const enemyGroup = this.physics.add.group([enemy1, enemy2, enemy3]);
   const bulletGroup = this.physics.add.group([bullet1, bullet2]);
   
   // Apply same collision behavior to all objects in groups
   this.physics.add.collider(enemyGroup, bulletGroup, this.handleCollision);
   ```

4. For Matter.js physics in Phaser, use collision filtering:
   ```typescript
   // Using Matter.js collision filtering in Phaser
   this.matter.body.setCollisionGroup(playerBody, 1);
   this.matter.body.setCollisionGroup(enemyBody, 2);
   
   // Set collision mask (what group this body can collide with)
   this.matter.body.setCollidesWith(playerBody, [2, 3]); // Collide with groups 2 and 3
   